The final validated outcome can be summarized as follows:
\begin{itemize}
    \item heterogeneous customer-related datasets can be ingested through a controlled Kafka path;
    \item raw data can be persisted in a replayable bronze layer on distributed HDFS;
    \item Spark can structure and load raw lakehouse tables in a distributed environment;
    \item Hive Metastore centralizes metadata for governed table access;
    \item Trino provides analytical SQL exposure over the lakehouse foundation;
    \item dbt-spark introduces modeled transformation discipline;
    \item Great Expectations provides quality-oriented evidence;
    \item Airflow provides explicit lifecycle orchestration;
    \item Prometheus and Grafana provide operational visibility;
    \item Kerberos, SPNEGO, TLS, authenticated gateways, and encrypted runtime storage strengthen the final deployment.
\end{itemize}

The most important interpretive result is that the platform can be defended as a \textbf{Customer 360-ready analytical foundation}, not as a finished enterprise AI product. This distinction is central to the academic honesty of the report. What the validation proves is that the complete engineering backbone is now operational: data can enter the platform, remain durably stored, be transformed through governed layers, be queried analytically, and be observed through dedicated monitoring interfaces. The retained results from
